	(text "The CAN+5V is a 5V rail that is separate from the main 5V rail that powers most of the logic.\nThis rail is meant to be stable, without all the switching and digital noise from the main 5V rail,\nand powers the VIC-II. Using the same 5V as all the chips could result in visible noise on the screen.\n\nThe CAN+5V also powers the color clock, and from the color clock the signal is derived to synchronize the\nmain +5V buck converter. The buck converter starts on its own oscillator and only locks to the color clock\nonce its output is good, so it does not depend on the CAN+5V to start. CAN+5V is generated from the 12 Volt\ninput, because a linear regulator can't make 5 V from a 5 V rail."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 149.86 41.656 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "89b3301f-86c7-4428-848f-6251c598427a")
	)
	(text "Diodes are used to reduce the input voltage on U801, transferring some of the generated heat\nfrom the regulator to the diodes.\n\nA linear regulator is used instead of a buck converter to avoid switching noise and ripple\nending up in the output.\n\nAt the worst-case load of about 360 mA, each diode dissipates about 0.3W and U801 about 1.2-1.4W.\nU801's tab is ground and should be connected to a large ground pour on both layers with vias, so\nno heat sink is needed."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 119.634 85.344 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "f1e2bf63-d658-45e0-8b68-2d05d08d12a0")
	)
